export const KNOWLEDGE = "# ABOUT YIMARU CONSULTING\nYimaru Consulting (part of Yimaru Academy) is a study-abroad consultancy for Ethiopian students. Office: Town Square Mall, Addis Ababa.\nContact: Telegram https://t.me/+251946396655 · WhatsApp https://wa.me/251946396655 · Phone +251946396655 · Email yimaruacademy@gmail.com.\nThe website has a free study plan (a short questionnaire that matches the student to countries) and pages for every country and scholarship below.\n\n# FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS\nQ: Is the first consultation really free? What happens in it?\nA: Yes, it is completely free, and there is no obligation to continue with us. You meet an advisor in our office in Addis Ababa or talk by phone. The advisor looks at your grades, your documents, your English level and your budget, and then tells you honestly:\n- which countries and universities fit you\n- which scholarships you have a real chance of winning\n- what is missing from your documents\n- a realistic timeline for your intakeBring whatever documents you have. Photos on your phone are fine for a first meeting.\nQ: Is the IELTS and Duolingo training really free?\nA: Yes. Every student who applies with Yimaru gets our IELTS and Duolingo English Test training for free. It is taught by the team behind Yimaru Academy.\n- We start with a level check, so you only study what you need.\n- You get lessons for listening, reading, writing and speaking, with feedback on your writing and speaking.\n- You take practice tests until you are ready for the real exam.The training is free. The exam fee itself is paid to the test provider (IELTS or Duolingo), not to us.\nQ: Should I take IELTS or Duolingo?\nA: It depends on where you are applying.\n- IELTS is accepted by almost every university and visa office. It is the safest choice if you are not sure.\n- Duolingo English Test is taken online from home, costs less and usually gives results in about two days. Thousands of universities accept it, but not all of them, and not every visa route.Most English-taught programs ask for IELTS 6.0 to 6.5, or about Duolingo 105 to 120. Before you book any test, we check the exact requirement of every university on your list.\nQ: Which documents do I need?\nA: Most applications from Ethiopia need:\n- a valid passport\n- Grade 12 certificate and national exam result (for a bachelor's)\n- degree certificate and official transcripts (for a master's or PhD)\n- an English test result (IELTS, Duolingo or TOEFL)\n- a CV, a motivation letter and usually two recommendation letters\n- proof of funds or a scholarship letter, for the visaCertificates usually need to be authenticated by the Ministry of Education and then the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, and some embassies also stamp them. Start early, because authentication can take weeks. We give you a checklist made for your countries.\nQ: How much does it cost to study abroad?\nA: Costs change a lot by country. Typical yearly figures:\n- Lower cost: Germany (no tuition at most public universities, but EUR 11,904 in a blocked account for living), India, Türkiye, Hungary, Russia and Malaysia.\n- Middle: Italy, France, China, South Korea and the UAE.\n- Higher: the United States, the United Kingdom, Canada and Australia.Full scholarships such as Stipendium Hungaricum, Türkiye Scholarships, CSC, GKS and ICCR can cover tuition and living costs. Every country page shows tuition and living costs, and your free study plan compares them with your budget.\nQ: How long does the whole process take?\nA: Most students need 3 to 8 months from the first consultation to the visa. A typical path:\n- English test preparation: 2 to 8 weeks, if needed\n- Choosing universities and preparing documents: 1 to 2 weeks\n- Applications: 1 to 2 months\n- Waiting for offers and scholarship results: 1 to 4 months\n- Visa: 1 to 3 months, depending on the embassyFor scholarships, start 9 to 12 months before the intake, because many close almost a year before classes begin.\nQ: Can I work while I study?\nA: In most countries, yes, within limits set by your student visa:\n- United Kingdom: up to 20 hours a week during term\n- Canada: up to 24 hours a week off campus during term\n- Australia: up to 48 hours per two weeks during term\n- Germany: 140 full days or 280 half days a year\n- United States: on campus only, up to 20 hours a week\n- India: regular paid work is not allowedPart-time work helps with living costs, but it is not enough to pay full tuition. Embassies want to see that you can pay without working. Each country page lists the rules in full.\nQ: Do you guarantee admission or a visa?\nA: No, and be careful with anyone who does. Universities decide admission and embassies decide visas. No agent can promise either. What we do promise is honest advice and a strong, complete application. Warning signs of a dishonest agent:\n- they guarantee a visa or admission\n- they offer fake bank statements, letters or certificates\n- they ask you to sign documents you do not understandFalse documents can get you banned from a country for years. We never use them.\nQ: I was refused a visa before. Can I still apply?\nA: Yes, in most cases. A refusal is not a ban. What matters is fixing the reason.\n- Bring your refusal letter. It states the reason.\n- Common reasons are weak proof of funds, unclear study plans, or doubts that you will follow the visa rules.\n- We help you fix each point with stronger documents and a clearer study plan before you reapply.You must always declare a previous refusal on a new application. Hiding it can lead to a much longer ban.\nQ: What if my grades are not strong?\nA: You still have real options. We tell you honestly what is realistic, then find the best route:\n- Foundation or pathway programs prepare you for a degree when your Grade 12 result is below the direct entry level.\n- Countries with more open entry, such as Hungary, Türkiye, Malaysia, Russia and India, accept a wider range of grades.\n- A strong English score and a clear motivation letter can balance a lower GPA.\n- For a master's, work experience counts, and some scholarships such as DAAD EPOS require it.\nQ: Will my degree be recognised in Ethiopia?\nA: We only shortlist accredited, government-recognised universities, which is the first condition for recognition at home. When you return, foreign degrees are evaluated by Ethiopia's Education and Training Authority. Keep your original certificates, transcripts and proof of study. Online-only or very short programs can be harder to recognise, so we check this before you apply.\n\n# DOCUMENTS EVERY STUDENT NEEDS (ETHIOPIA)\n- Grade 12 certificate (and transcripts/degree for master's), authenticated at ETA (education authority) and then the Ministry of Foreign Affairs.\n- A valid passport. Apply early, it can take months.\n- Proof of funds (bank statements), usually showing the money has been in the account for some months.\n- Police certificate where the country asks for it: In Ethiopia, the Certificate of Good Conduct comes from the Federal Police Forensic Investigation Department. Apply in person; your fingerprints are taken.\n\n# SCAM WARNINGS\n- No agent can guarantee a visa or sell embassy appointments. Embassies such as Poland's in Addis Ababa say they do not work with agents.\n- Use only official embassy websites and emails. Scammers make look-alike sites.\n- Never use a fake bank statement or document. Embassies check them; it leads to refusal and can block future visas.\n- Pay tuition only to the university's own account, after confirming your admission letter with the university directly.\n- Be careful with 'study and work' or job offers abroad that ask for money up front. Ethiopian authorities have warned about these and taken fake agents to court.\n- Government scholarships are applied for on their official websites. Do not pay anyone to 'secure' one for you.\n\n# DESTINATIONS\n## United States\nSummary: Up to three years of work after STEM degrees. Home to many of the world's top-ranked universities, with merit scholarships at many of them and wide acceptance of the Duolingo English Test. OPT gives 12 months of work after graduation, up to 36 on a STEM degree.\nKey point: Home to many of the world's top-ranked universities, with programs in almost every subject.\nTuition: $15,000–70,000/yr. Living: $1,200–2,500/mo. English: IELTS 6.5 / DET 110+. Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $24,000. Minimum English: IELTS 6.5 or equal.\nFacts: tuition USD 15,000–70,000/yr; living USD 1,200–2,500/month by city; intakes August/September · January; processing 2 to 8+ weeks including interview wait; language English; IELTS 6.5+, TOEFL 80+ or Duolingo 110+.\nExample universities: Arizona State University, University of Arizona, Iowa State University, University of South Florida, Texas Tech University, Minnesota State University. About 2,600+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Computer Science, Engineering, Business, Public Health.\nVisa: F-1 Student Visa. Steps: 1) Get admitted and receive Form I-20 2) Pay the SEVIS fee (USD 350) and the visa fee (USD 185), and complete the DS-160 3) Attend the interview at the U.S. Embassy in Addis Ababa 4) Make your social media accounts public and list those from the last five years 5) Show one year of funds and strong ties to home; a new USD 250 visa integrity fee may be charged when the visa is issued\nPaid or shown before the visa: No tuition in advance. The SEVIS fee ($350), the visa fee ($185) and the new $250 visa integrity fee, which embassies have started charging when the visa is issued.\nExtra documents and health checks: No medical exam or police certificate is needed for the student visa.\nVisa tip: Explain clearly how this degree helps your career back in Ethiopia. Answer in your own words; officers notice memorised answers.\nWork while studying (limited): On campus up to 20 hours a week; internships through CPT; OPT for 12 months after graduation, up to 36 on a STEM degree.\nEarnings: Only on-campus jobs during your studies, up to 20 hours a week in term. Pay depends on the state and the university.\nStaying after graduation: good (12–36 months (OPT)).\nOPT note: OPT still exists, but in October 2026 the US government proposed large new fees for it. This is not in effect yet. We confirm the latest when you talk to us.\nTips: Ethiopian students can still apply for F-1 visas. The 2026 visa bond for Ethiopians applies to tourist and business visas, not student visas. Consular officers review your public social media from the last five years. Many universities accept the Duolingo English Test, taken from home.\nScholarships: Fulbright Foreign Student Program, Mastercard Foundation Scholars Program.\n\n## Canada\nSummary: Work while you study, strong graduate work rights. Canada lets students work 24 hours a week off campus and offers a Post-Graduation Work Permit of up to three years. Study permits are capped, and from 1 September 2026 you must show at least CAD 23,448 a year for living costs, plus tuition and travel.\nKey point: Work up to 24 hours a week off campus, and stay up to 3 years after graduating.\nTuition: CAD 18,000–45,000/yr. Living: CAD 1,700–2,700/mo. English: IELTS 6.5. Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $29,000. Minimum English: IELTS 6.5 or equal.\nFacts: tuition CAD 18,000–45,000/yr; living CAD 1,700–2,700/month; intakes September · January; processing Varies widely by country; language English or French; IELTS 6.5+.\nExample universities: University of Manitoba, University of Regina, Memorial University, University of Saskatchewan, University of Toronto, University of British Columbia. About 95+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Business, Engineering, Computer Science, Health Sciences.\nVisa: Study Permit (with a Provincial Attestation Letter for most). Steps: 1) Get a Letter of Acceptance from a Designated Learning Institution 2) Get a Provincial Attestation Letter (master's and PhD students at public institutions are exempt since January 2026) 3) Prove living funds of at least CAD 23,448 for the first year, plus tuition and travel 4) Give biometrics; medical exam if required 5) Apply online through IRCC\nPaid or shown before the visa: Proof of at least CAD 23,448 for living costs, your first semester or year of tuition (about CAD 8,000 to 18,000) paid in advance, which strongly helps the visa, and CAD 235 in permit and biometrics fees.\nExtra documents and health checks: An immigration medical exam with a panel physician approved by Canada. It is required for people living in Ethiopia.\nVisa tip: Show 6 months of clean bank history and where the money came from (business licence, tax records, property). Paying first-year tuition in advance and a strong study plan help a lot.\nWork while studying (strong): Up to 24 hours a week off campus during term, full-time in breaks. The Post-Graduation Work Permit runs 8 months to 3 years, and degree graduates need CLB 7 English in all four skills to get it.\nEarnings: Minimum wage depends on the province: CAD 17.95 an hour in Ontario, CAD 18.25 in British Columbia, CAD 15 in Alberta. At 24 hours a week in Ontario, roughly CAD 1,850 a month before tax.\nStaying after graduation: strong (8 months–3 years).\nTips: Check your program is eligible for the Post-Graduation Work Permit before enrolling. Mastercard Foundation Scholars places are fully funded but very competitive.\nScholarships: Mastercard Foundation Scholars Program.\n\n## United Kingdom\nSummary: Up to 2 years of work after you graduate (18 months from 2027). One-year master's degrees and world-famous universities. After graduating, the Graduate Route lets you stay and work with no salary requirement: 18 months for applications from 1 January 2027 (3 years after a PhD). The health surcharge is paid up front.\nKey point: Most bachelor's degrees take 3 years and master's 1 year, so you finish sooner. Graduates can stay to work.\nTuition: £15,000–40,000/yr. Living: £1,000–1,350/mo. English: IELTS 6.0–7.0. Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $28,500. Minimum English: IELTS 6 or equal.\nFacts: tuition GBP 15,000–40,000/yr; living GBP 1,203/month outside London · 1,570 in London (visa rule from 30 Nov 2026); intakes September/October · January at many; processing About 3 weeks; priority options; language English; UKVI IELTS 6.0–7.0.\nExample universities: University of Manchester, University of Leeds, Coventry University, University of Hertfordshire, Sheffield Hallam University, University of East London. About 160+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Business & Management, Engineering, Computer Science & AI, Public Health.\nVisa: Student visa (points-based). Steps: 1) Accept an unconditional offer and get your CAS 2) Hold maintenance funds for 28 days in a row: GBP 1,203 a month outside London or 1,570 in London, for up to 9 months (applications from 30 November 2026) 3) Apply online; pay the GBP 558 visa fee and the GBP 776-a-year Immigration Health Surcharge 4) Give biometrics at the visa centre in Addis Ababa 5) Receive your eVisa\nPaid or shown before the visa: A tuition deposit of about £3,000 to £6,000 to get your CAS, the health surcharge of £776 a year and the £558 visa fee.\nExtra documents and health checks: A TB test at an approved clinic in Addis Ababa (IOM or the American Medical Center), because you are coming for 6 months or more.\nVisa tip: Your balance must stay at or above the required amount every day for 28 days. Check the daily balance before you print the statement, and practise for the credibility interview.\nWork while studying (good): 20 hours a week during term, full-time in holidays. Then the Graduate Route: 2 years if you apply by 31 December 2026, 18 months from 1 January 2027, 3 years after a PhD.\nEarnings: Minimum wage from April 2026: £12.71 an hour at 21 or older, £10.85 at 18 to 20. At 20 hours a week that is roughly £1,100 a month before tax.\nStaying after graduation: good (18 months–2 years).\nGrade 12 route: Your Grade 12 certificate usually needs a foundation year first.\nTips: The health surcharge is GBP 776 for each year of your visa, paid when you apply. Chevening is fully funded but expects at least two years of work experience.\nScholarships: Chevening Scholarship, Mastercard Foundation Scholars Program.\n\n## Germany\nSummary: Tuition-free public universities. Most public universities charge no tuition, only a semester fee, and there are 2,000+ English-taught programs. You need a blocked account of €11,904 for your first year, and graduates get 18 months to find a job.\nKey point: Most public universities charge no tuition, even for international students. Graduates get 18 months to find a job.\nTuition: €0 + semester fee. Living: €992+/mo. English: IELTS 6.0–6.5. Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $13,500. Minimum English: IELTS 6 or equal.\nFacts: tuition EUR 0 at most public universities · semester fee EUR 150–400 · Baden-Württemberg EUR 1,500/semester; living EUR 992/month (blocked account EUR 11,904/yr); intakes October (winter) · April (summer); processing Book the embassy appointment early; often 6–12 weeks; language German for most bachelor's; 2,000+ English-taught master's.\nExample universities: Technical University of Munich, RWTH Aachen University, TU Berlin, University of Göttingen, University of Freiburg, Rhine-Waal University of Applied Sciences. About 400+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Engineering, Computer Science, Data Science, Public Health, Agriculture.\nVisa: National Type D Student Visa. Steps: 1) Apply to universities directly or through uni-assist 2) Open a blocked account with EUR 11,904 for the first year 3) Get health insurance 4) Attend your appointment at the German Embassy in Addis Ababa 5) Register your address and get your residence permit after arrival\nPaid or shown before the visa: €11,904 in a blocked account, health insurance of about €120 a month and the €75 visa fee. Public universities charge no tuition.\nVisa tip: Check every Ministry of Education and Foreign Affairs stamp on your documents, book real accommodation, and know your course well for the interview.\nWork while studying (good): You can work 140 full days or 280 half days a year. After graduating, an 18-month permit lets you look for work.\nEarnings: Minimum wage €13.90 an hour in 2026. 20 hours a week is roughly €1,200 a month before tax, within the 140 full days a year limit.\nStaying after graduation: strong (18 months).\nGrade 12 route: Germany usually asks for a year of university in Ethiopia first, or a Studienkolleg (a preparation year) in Germany.\nTips: German embassy appointments fill up fast. Book as soon as you apply. A little German helps a lot with part-time jobs, even on English programs.\nScholarships: DAAD Development-Related Postgraduate Courses (EPOS), Erasmus Mundus Joint Masters.\n\n## Italy\nSummary: Low, income-based tuition and strong grants. Italian public university fees are capped by law and scaled to family income (the ISEE system), so many international students pay close to the €900 floor. There are 500+ English-taught degrees, mostly at master's level.\nKey point: Affordable public universities, English-taught medicine and engineering, and regional grants for students with lower family income.\nTuition: €900–4,000/yr. Living: €700–1,300/mo. English: IELTS 5.5–6.5. Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $8,700. Minimum English: IELTS 6 or equal.\nFacts: tuition EUR 900–4,000/yr public · EUR 6,000–20,000/yr private; living EUR 700–1,300/month; intakes September/October · some February/March; processing 3–6 weeks · 8–10 weeks in summer; language Italian for most bachelor's; 500+ English-taught programs.\nExample universities: Politecnico di Milano, University of Bologna, University of Padua, Sapienza University of Rome, Politecnico di Torino, University of Pavia. About 90+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Engineering, Architecture & Design, Business & Economics, Medicine (IMAT).\nVisa: National Type D Study Visa (Visto per Studio). Steps: 1) Get admitted, then pre-enrol on the Universitaly portal 2) Legalise certificates at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs; get a Declaration of Value or CIMEA statement 3) Prove funds of at least EUR 10,180 a year (2026/27 and 2027/28 rule) 4) Arrange health insurance and proof of accommodation 5) Apply for the Type D visa at the Italian Embassy in Addis Ababa\nPaid or shown before the visa: No tuition in advance. Only application fees (€0 to €30), CIMEA and translation costs, travel and health insurance (about €150 to €400) and the €50 visa fee.\nVisa tip: Check every Ministry of Education and Foreign Affairs stamp on your documents, book real accommodation, and know your course well for the interview.\nWork while studying (good): Up to 20 hours a week during term, full-time in holidays. After graduating, a 12-month permit lets you stay and look for work.\nEarnings: There is no national minimum wage; pay is set by sector agreements.\nStaying after graduation: good (12 months).\nTips: Fees are income-based, so two students on the same course can pay very different tuition. DSU grants can include free housing and meals worth more than the tuition.\nScholarships: Italy regional study grant (DSU), Erasmus Mundus Joint Masters.\n\n## Hungary\nSummary: Stipendium Hungaricum covers tuition, stipend and housing. Hungary runs Stipendium Hungaricum, one of the world's largest fully funded scholarships, covering tuition, a monthly stipend and housing at 25+ public universities. It is also a major hub for English-taught medicine.\nKey point: Every year, the Stipendium Hungaricum full scholarship offers places to Ethiopian students.\nTuition: €3,000–7,500/yr. Living: €500–700/mo. English: IELTS 5.5–6.5. Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $8,000. Minimum English: IELTS 5.5 or equal.\nFacts: tuition EUR 3,000–7,500/yr · medicine EUR 16,000–18,000; living EUR 500–700/month; intakes September · some February; processing 4–8 weeks, then residence permit on arrival; language Mostly English-taught; IELTS 5.5–6.5.\nExample universities: University of Debrecen, University of Pécs, University of Szeged, Eötvös Loránd University (ELTE), Budapest University of Technology and Economics, Semmelweis University. About 60+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Medicine & Dentistry, Engineering, Business & Economics, Computer Science.\nVisa: National Type D Visa, then a residence permit. Steps: 1) Get your acceptance or Stipendium Hungaricum placement letter 2) Show funds of roughly HUF 200,000 a month plus health insurance 3) Apply for the Type D visa with accommodation proof and police clearance 4) Travel, then apply for the residence permit 5) Register your address and give biometrics\nPaid or shown before the visa: At private universities, your first semester or first year of tuition is usually paid before the visa.\nVisa tip: Check every Ministry of Education and Foreign Affairs stamp on your documents, book real accommodation, and know your course well for the interview.\nWork while studying (good): Up to 30 hours a week during semesters, and full-time for up to 90 days a year outside semesters. Graduates can move to a job-seeker permit or an EU Blue Card.\nEarnings: Minimum wage about €840 a month full-time in 2026. At 30 hours a week, roughly €600 a month before tax.\nStaying after graduation: good (Job-seeker permit).\nTips: Stipendium Hungaricum gives places by country quota, so apply early. Self-funded students often must show a large amount of money up front.\nScholarships: Stipendium Hungaricum, Erasmus Mundus Joint Masters.\n\n## China\nSummary: Top universities and full CSC scholarships at low cost. Chinese universities are climbing world rankings while tuition stays low, and the Chinese Government Scholarship (CSC) fully funds many international students. English-taught programs are growing, but campus life is mostly in Mandarin.\nKey point: Very affordable tuition, Chinese Government Scholarships, and many English-taught medical programs.\nTuition: CNY 20,000–50,000/yr. Living: CNY 2,000–5,500/mo. English: IELTS 6.0. Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $5,000. Minimum English: IELTS 6 or equal.\nFacts: tuition CNY 20,000–50,000/yr (≈ USD 2,800–7,000); living CNY 2,000–5,500/month; intakes September · March; processing 4–7 business days; language English programs IELTS 6.0; Chinese programs HSK 4–5.\nExample universities: Zhejiang University, Wuhan University, Beijing Institute of Technology, Nanjing University, Tianjin University, Southeast University. About 1,300+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Engineering, Business, Medicine, Computer Science.\nVisa: X1 Student Visa. Steps: 1) Get the Admission Notice plus JW201 (scholarship) or JW202 (self-funded) 2) Complete the Foreigner Physical Examination Form 3) Apply at the Chinese Embassy in Addis Ababa 4) Convert to a residence permit within 30 days of arrival\nExtra documents and health checks: The Foreigner Physical Examination Form, done at a recognised hospital within 6 months of applying. Carry your yellow fever card too.\nWork while studying (very limited): Part-time work needs university approval and a permit. No automatic post-study visa; graduates need a job offer.\nEarnings: Paid work is very limited, so plan to be fully funded.\nStaying after graduation: limited (Job offer needed).\nTips: Submitting the wrong JW form is a common, avoidable visa mistake. Provincial scholarships are less competitive than the national CSC.\nScholarships: Chinese Government Scholarship (CSC).\n\n## Türkiye\nSummary: Full Türkiye Scholarships, close to home. Türkiye Scholarships (Türkiye Bursları) fully fund thousands of international students every year, including a Turkish language year. Public universities are affordable, many programs are taught in English, and Istanbul is about five and a half hours from Addis.\nKey point: Close to home and affordable. Türkiye Scholarships cover tuition, housing, a stipend, health insurance and your flight.\nTuition: $1,000–6,000/yr. Living: $400–750/mo. English: IELTS 5.5–6.5. Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $4,600. Minimum English: IELTS 5.5 or equal.\nFacts: tuition USD 1,000–6,000/yr public · USD 5,000–20,000/yr private; living USD 400–750/month; intakes September · some February; processing Student visa from the Turkish Embassy, then a residence permit within one month; language English or Turkish; Turkish-taught programs include a prep year.\nExample universities: Middle East Technical University (METU), Istanbul Technical University, Boğaziçi University, Istanbul University, Hacettepe University, Ankara University. About 200+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Medicine, Engineering, International Relations, Business, Architecture.\nVisa: Student visa, then a residence permit (ikamet). Steps: 1) Get your admission letter or Türkiye Scholarships award 2) Apply for the student visa at the Turkish Embassy in Addis Ababa 3) Travel and register at your university 4) Apply for your residence permit within one month 5) Get your Turkish ID number and health insurance\nPaid or shown before the visa: At private universities, a deposit of about $500 to $2,000, plus insurance.\nWork while studying (limited): Students can work part-time with a work permit. Bachelor's students usually qualify after their first year, up to 24 hours a week.\nEarnings: Work needs a permit and is usually allowed from the second year, so do not count on it for your first-year costs.\nStaying after graduation: limited (Job offer needed).\nTips: Türkiye Scholarships opens once a year, usually January to February. Prepare your documents before it opens. Many scholarship students study in Turkish after a free language year.\nScholarships: Türkiye Scholarships (Türkiye Bursları).\n\n## Russia\nSummary: Low tuition and government scholarship quotas. Tuition is a fraction of Western costs, and the Russian Government offers scholarship quotas to Ethiopian students. Most programs are taught in Russian after a one-year language course; English tracks exist mainly in medicine and engineering.\nKey point: Low tuition, government quota places that cover tuition, and a one-year Russian language course before your degree.\nTuition: $1,600–8,000/yr. Living: $325–650/mo. English: Prep year or IELTS 6.0. Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $4,500. Minimum English: IELTS 5.5 or equal.\nFacts: tuition USD 1,600–8,000/yr · medicine up to ~16,000; living USD 325–650/month; intakes September · February; processing 10–20 working days; language Mostly Russian after a prep year; English tracks IELTS 6.0.\nExample universities: RUDN University, Kazan Federal University, HSE University, Saint Petersburg State University, Tomsk Polytechnic University, Ural Federal University. About 700+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Medicine, Engineering, Computer Science, International Relations.\nVisa: Russian Student Visa. Steps: 1) Get admitted and receive the invitation letter (about a month) 2) Get an HIV test certificate dated within three months 3) Arrange medical insurance for your whole stay 4) Apply at the Russian Embassy in Addis Ababa 5) Register with migration authorities within 7 working days of arrival\nExtra documents and health checks: A negative HIV test from the last 3 months for the visa. After arrival, a medical check and fingerprints within 90 days, repeated every year.\nWork while studying (very limited): Limited. Part-time work needs a separate permit, and most international students do not work.\nEarnings: Paid work is very limited, so plan to be fully funded.\nStaying after graduation: limited (Job offer needed).\nTips: Missing the 7-day registration after arrival can lead to fines. Check that your degree will be recognised in Ethiopia before you enrol.\nScholarships: Russian Government Scholarship.\n\n## United Arab Emirates\nSummary: A Western degree, three hours from Addis. The UAE hosts branch campuses of UK, Australian and US universities, so you can earn a recognised degree close to home, with a large Ethiopian community in Dubai. Families often choose it for safety and proximity.\nKey point: About a 4-hour flight from Addis Ababa, with branch campuses of international universities and a large Ethiopian community.\nTuition: $5,000–35,000/yr. Living: $700–1,400/mo. English: IELTS 6.0–6.5. Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $14,000. Minimum English: IELTS 6 or equal.\nFacts: tuition USD 5,000–35,000/yr · branch campuses 15,000–25,000; living USD 700–1,400/month; intakes September · January/February; processing Entry permit 2–4 weeks; language English; IELTS 6.0–6.5.\nExample universities: Heriot-Watt University Dubai, Middlesex University Dubai, University of Wollongong in Dubai, University of Birmingham Dubai, Canadian University Dubai, American University of Sharjah. About 90+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Business, Engineering, Computer Science & AI, Hospitality & Tourism.\nVisa: UAE Student Residence Visa (sponsored by the university). Steps: 1) Get an offer and sponsorship letter from an accredited UAE university 2) The university files your entry permit 3) Medical fitness test within 30 days of arrival 4) Emirates ID biometrics and health insurance 5) Residence visa issued within 60 days\nWork while studying (limited): Part-time work needs a MoHRE work permit and your university's approval, often 15 to 20 hours a week in term and full-time in official breaks. After graduation, an employer-sponsored work visa.\nEarnings: There is no general minimum wage, and work needs a permit and your university's approval.\nStaying after graduation: limited (Job offer needed).\nTips: Most routes are through private or branch campuses. A medical test is done in the UAE after arrival.\n\n## France\nSummary: Low public fees and the Eiffel scholarship. From 2026-27, almost all new non-EU students at public universities pay EUR 2,895 a year for a bachelor's and EUR 3,941 for a master's, still far below most English-speaking countries. Most bachelor's are in French, with many English-taught master's.\nKey point: Public university fees are much lower than in the UK or USA, and the Eiffel scholarship supports top master's and PhD students.\nTuition: €2,895–3,941/yr. Living: €800–1,300/mo. English: IELTS 6.0–6.5. Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $10,000. Minimum English: IELTS 6 or equal.\nFacts: tuition EUR 2,895/yr bachelor's · EUR 3,941/yr master's at public universities · grandes écoles cost more; living EUR 800–1,300/month (Paris higher); intakes September · some January; processing Campus France interview, then the visa in about 2–4 weeks; language French for most bachelor's; many English-taught master's.\nExample universities: Sorbonne University, Université Paris-Saclay, Sciences Po, Université Grenoble Alpes, INSA Lyon, University of Strasbourg. About 70+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Engineering, Business & Management, Data Science, International Relations, Hospitality.\nVisa: Long-stay student visa (VLS-TS). Steps: 1) Apply through the Campus France 'Études en France' platform 2) Attend the Campus France interview 3) Show funds of at least EUR 877.50 a month (rule since 1 August 2026) 4) Submit your visa application for the French Embassy in Addis Ababa 5) Validate your VLS-TS online within three months of arrival\nVisa tip: Check every Ministry of Education and Foreign Affairs stamp on your documents, book real accommodation, and know your course well for the interview.\nWork while studying (good): You can work up to 964 hours a year, about 20 hours a week. After a master's, you can get a one-year permit to look for work.\nEarnings: Minimum wage about €12 an hour (€1,823 a month full-time in January 2026). The 964 hours a year limit is roughly €950 a month before tax.\nStaying after graduation: good (12 months after a master's).\nTips: Even basic French makes daily life and part-time work much easier. Your French university applies for the Eiffel scholarship for you, so apply for admission early.\nScholarships: France Excellence Eiffel Scholarship, Erasmus Mundus Joint Masters.\n\n## South Korea\nSummary: GKS: a full government scholarship with a language year. Korea combines top technology universities with the Global Korea Scholarship (GKS), which covers tuition, flights, a monthly allowance and a year of Korean. Ethiopia has its own embassy-track places every year.\nKey point: Strong in engineering and technology. The Global Korea Scholarship covers tuition, living costs, flights and a Korean language year.\nTuition: KRW 6–12M/yr. Living: KRW 0.9–1.4M/mo. English: IELTS 5.5–6.5. Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $12,000. Minimum English: IELTS 6 or equal.\nFacts: tuition KRW 6–12 million/yr (about USD 4,000–9,000); living KRW 0.9–1.4 million/month; intakes March · September; processing D-2 visa usually 2–4 weeks after admission; language Korean or English; many graduate programs in English.\nExample universities: Seoul National University, KAIST, Korea University, Yonsei University, POSTECH, Kyung Hee University. About 190+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Engineering, Computer Science & AI, Business, International Studies, Biotechnology.\nVisa: Student visa (D-2). Steps: 1) Get your admission letter or GKS placement 2) Notarise documents at MOFA Ethiopia, then at the Korean Embassy consular section 3) Show proof of funds or your scholarship letter 4) Apply for the D-2 visa at the Korean Embassy in Addis Ababa 5) Get your residence card within 90 days of arrival\nExtra documents and health checks: A TB screening certificate from a hospital chosen by the Korean embassy. It is valid for 3 months.\nWork while studying (limited): Students need a free part-time work permit first. Hours depend on your level and Korean (TOPIK): master's and PhD students with TOPIK 4 can work up to 30 hours a week, others less. After graduating, the D-10 job-seeker visa.\nEarnings: Minimum wage KRW 10,320 an hour in 2026 (KRW 10,700 in 2027). At 20 hours a week, roughly KRW 890,000 a month before tax.\nStaying after graduation: good (Job-seeker visa).\nTips: GKS embassy-track places for Ethiopia are few, so a strong study plan matters. Learning basic Korean early helps with daily life and part-time jobs.\nScholarships: Global Korea Scholarship (GKS).\n\n## India\nSummary: Low costs and ICCR scholarships set aside for Ethiopians. India is one of the most affordable places to study in English, and many Ethiopians have studied there. Through the ICCR India-Africa Maitri scheme, India offers a set number of fully funded places to Ethiopians each year.\nKey point: One of the most affordable options, with ICCR scholarships open to Ethiopian students and classes in English.\nTuition: $1,500–8,000/yr. Living: $200–450/mo. English: IELTS 5.5–6.0. Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $4,000. Minimum English: IELTS 5.5 or equal.\nFacts: tuition USD 1,500–8,000/yr; living USD 200–450/month; intakes July/August; processing Student visa usually 2–4 weeks; language English.\nExample universities: University of Delhi, Jawaharlal Nehru University, Savitribai Phule Pune University, Osmania University, Manipal Academy of Higher Education, Amity University. About 1,100+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Engineering, Computer Science, Pharmacy, Business (MBA), Agriculture.\nVisa: Student visa (or e-Student visa). Steps: 1) Get your admission letter or ICCR award 2) Apply online for the Indian student visa 3) Submit documents to the Indian visa centre or Embassy in Addis Ababa 4) Travel and register online with the FRRO within 14 days if required\nExtra documents and health checks: An original yellow fever vaccination card, taken at least 10 days before you fly. Without it you can be quarantined for up to 6 days.\nWork while studying (very limited): Student visas do not allow regular paid work. Internships linked to your course may be allowed.\nEarnings: Paid work is very limited, so plan to be fully funded.\nStaying after graduation: very limited (Job offer needed).\nTips: ICCR applications open once a year on the A2A portal, usually February to April. Check that the university is UGC-recognised before you accept an offer.\nScholarships: ICCR India-Africa Maitri Scholarship.\n\n## Malaysia\nSummary: English-taught degrees at Asian prices. Almost everything is taught in English, costs are low, and you can earn a UK or Australian degree at a branch campus in Kuala Lumpur. Your university applies for your Student Pass for you.\nKey point: Affordable, English-taught programs, including branch campuses of well-known UK and Australian universities.\nTuition: MYR 15,000–60,000/yr. Living: MYR 1,500–2,500/mo. English: IELTS 5.5–6.0. Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $7,000. Minimum English: IELTS 5.5 or equal.\nFacts: tuition MYR 15,000–60,000/yr (about USD 3,500–13,000); living MYR 1,500–2,500/month; intakes February/March · September/October; processing Student Pass approval through EMGS, usually 3–8 weeks; language English.\nExample universities: Universiti Malaya, Universiti Putra Malaysia, Universiti Teknologi Malaysia, Taylor's University, Monash University Malaysia, Asia Pacific University (APU). About 80+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Computer Science & IT, Business, Engineering, Hospitality, Medicine.\nVisa: Student Pass (through EMGS) with a single-entry visa. Steps: 1) Get your offer letter 2) Your university applies for your Student Pass through EMGS 3) Receive your visa approval letter (VAL) 4) Get your single-entry visa and travel 5) Complete the medical check and Student Pass sticker in Malaysia\nPaid or shown before the visa: A deposit of about $500 to $2,000, plus the EMGS visa processing and insurance fees.\nExtra documents and health checks: A yellow fever vaccination card, and a medical check at an EMGS panel clinic within 7 working days of arriving.\nWork while studying (limited): Students can work up to 20 hours a week, only during semester breaks longer than seven days.\nEarnings: Minimum wage about RM8.72 an hour, but students may only work in semester breaks.\nStaying after graduation: limited (Job offer needed).\nTips: Check that the university is registered with EMGS before you commit. Branch campuses give a foreign degree at a lower cost.\n\n## Australia\nSummary: Work 48 hours a fortnight, and 2+ years after you graduate. World-ranked universities, strong part-time work rights and a post-study work visa of two years or more. It is one of the more expensive options, and visa officers look closely at your Genuine Student statement and funds.\nKey point: Work up to 48 hours per fortnight while studying, and stay 2 to 3 years after graduating.\nTuition: AUD 25,000–50,000/yr. Living: AUD 2,000–2,800/mo. English: IELTS 6.0–6.5. Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $32,000. Minimum English: IELTS 6.5 or equal.\nFacts: tuition AUD 25,000–50,000/yr; living AUD 29,710/yr must be shown for the visa; intakes February · July; processing Varies; apply at least 3 months before your course starts; language English.\nExample universities: University of Melbourne, University of Sydney, Monash University, UNSW Sydney, University of Queensland, University of Adelaide. About 40+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Engineering, Nursing & Health, IT, Business & Accounting, Agriculture.\nVisa: Student visa (subclass 500). Steps: 1) Get your offer and Confirmation of Enrolment (CoE) 2) Buy Overseas Student Health Cover (OSHC) 3) Write your Genuine Student statement and prepare proof of funds 4) Apply online through ImmiAccount and give biometrics 5) Do the health check if you are asked to\nExtra documents and health checks: A health exam with an approved panel doctor: medical check, chest X-ray and blood tests.\nWork while studying (strong): Up to 48 hours a fortnight during term, and unlimited in breaks. After graduating, the Temporary Graduate visa (485) gives 2 years after a bachelor's or coursework master's, and 3 after a research degree or PhD.\nEarnings: National minimum wage AUD 26.44 an hour from July 2026. 48 hours a fortnight is roughly AUD 2,750 a month before tax.\nStaying after graduation: strong (2–3 years).\nTips: The student visa fee is AUD 2,500 from 1 July 2026, so make sure your file is complete before you apply. The post-study 485 visa now costs AUD 4,600, so plan for it in your budget. Regional universities can be cheaper and give extra post-study work time.\n\n## Austria\nSummary: Public universities usually waive tuition for Ethiopian citizens. Austria's public universities charge non-EU students €726.72 a semester, but citizens of the least developed countries, including Ethiopia, are usually exempt and pay only the student union fee. Most bachelor's degrees are taught in German, while more master's are in English. Graduates get 12 months to look for a job.\nKey point: Public universities usually waive tuition for Ethiopian citizens, so you mainly pay the student union fee of about €25 a semester.\nTuition: €0–727/semester. Living: €950–1,100/mo. English: IELTS 6.0–6.5 / German B2. Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $11,500. Minimum English: IELTS 6 or equal.\nFacts: tuition Public universities EUR 726.72/semester, usually waived for Ethiopian citizens (student union fee about EUR 25) · universities of applied sciences and private universities charge more; living EUR 950–1,100/month · proof of funds EUR 722.58/month under 24, EUR 1,308.39 from 24; intakes October (winter semester) · March (summer semester); processing The residence permit decision can take up to 90 days; language German for most bachelor's; more English-taught master's.\nExample universities: University of Vienna, TU Wien, University of Graz, University of Innsbruck, Johannes Kepler University Linz, BOKU University. About 75+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Engineering, Computer Science, Music & Arts, Business & Economics, Life Sciences.\nVisa: Residence Permit – Student (applied for at the Austrian Embassy in Addis Ababa). Steps: 1) Get admitted by an Austrian university or university of applied sciences 2) Legalise your certificates and get sworn translations 3) Show funds for 12 months: EUR 722.58 a month under 24, EUR 1,308.39 from 24 4) Apply in person for the Residence Permit – Student at the Austrian Embassy in Addis Ababa 5) Enter on the Visa D and collect your residence permit in Austria\nPaid or shown before the visa: Funds for 12 months: €722.58 a month if you are under 24, or €1,308.39 if you are 24 or older, plus more if your rent is high. Health insurance is also required.\nVisa tip: Check every Ministry of Education and Foreign Affairs stamp on your documents, book real accommodation, and know your course well for the interview.\nWork while studying (good): Up to 20 hours a week. After graduating, a 12-month permit lets you look for a job, and you can then switch to a Red-White-Red Card.\nEarnings: There is no national minimum wage; pay is set by sector agreements.\nStaying after graduation: good (12 months).\nGrade 12 route: Many universities here ask for a year of university study in Ethiopia after Grade 12, or a preparation course.\nTips: Ask your university about the tuition waiver for citizens of least developed countries. It is not always automatic. Plan for German. Most bachelor's degrees are taught in German.\nScholarships: Erhard Busek Grant (Austria), Erasmus Mundus Joint Masters.\n\n## Poland\nSummary: Affordable English-taught degrees in the EU. Poland offers affordable English-taught degrees and lower living costs than Western Europe. Since mid-2025, first-year bachelor's students need a NAWA document confirming their Grade 12 certificate before the visa. Full-time students at approved universities can work without a permit, and graduates get 9 months to look for work.\nKey point: Affordable English-taught degrees, and graduates of full-time studies can work without a work permit.\nTuition: €2,000–6,000/yr. Living: PLN 2,500–4,500/mo. English: B2 English (IELTS 5.5–6.5). Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $8,000. Minimum English: IELTS 5.5 or equal.\nFacts: tuition EUR 2,000–6,000/yr for most English-taught bachelor's · medicine EUR 10,000–16,000; living PLN 2,500–4,500/month; intakes October · some February; processing Decision usually within 15 days (up to 30); appointments are scarce in peak season; language English-taught programs need B2 English.\nExample universities: University of Warsaw, Warsaw University of Technology, Jagiellonian University, AGH University of Krakow, Poznań University of Technology, Medical University of Gdańsk. About 340+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Medicine & Dentistry, Engineering, Computer Science, Business & Management.\nVisa: National Type D Student Visa (Embassy of Poland, Addis Ababa). Steps: 1) Get admitted by an eligible Polish university 2) Get NAWA's written information (or a recognition decision) on your Grade 12 certificate 3) Superlegalise your documents and prepare proof of B2 English 4) Register on e-Konsulat and apply in person; the fee is USD 235 in cash 5) Apply for a temporary residence permit after arriving\nPaid or shown before the visa: Living funds of PLN 776 a month plus rent, PLN 2,500 for your return trip, the USD 235 visa fee paid in cash, and often your first semester or year of tuition.\nExtra documents and health checks: Travel medical insurance of at least €30,000. No medical or police certificate is on the embassy's list.\nVisa tip: Book only through e-Konsulat; no agent can get you an appointment. Bring the NAWA document for your Grade 12 certificate, or the visa is refused.\nWork while studying (good): Full-time students at approved universities can work without a work permit and with no fixed hour limit. After graduating, a 9-month permit lets you look for work, and graduates of full-time Polish studies can work without a permit.\nEarnings: Minimum wage PLN 31.40 an hour in 2026. At 20 hours a week, roughly PLN 2,700 a month before tax.\nStaying after graduation: good (9 months).\nGrade 12 route: Poland needs NAWA's written confirmation that your Grade 12 certificate gives access to university, before the visa.\nTips: The Polish embassy in Addis Ababa does not work with agents. Follow its official Telegram channel, Study in Poland! Ethiopia. Check that your university is approved. Students at other schools may not be allowed to work.\nScholarships: Ignacy Łukasiewicz Scholarship (Poland), Erasmus Mundus Joint Masters.\n\n## Greece\nSummary: Low living costs, with English-taught programs growing. Greece has public universities across the country and, since 2025, new private ones. Most bachelor's degrees are taught in Greek, but English-taught options are growing, especially at master's level. Students can work part-time, but there is no special permit to stay and look for work after graduating.\nKey point: Low living costs, but most bachelor's degrees are in Greek. English options are growing, especially for master's.\nTuition: €1,500–7,000/yr. Living: €700–1,200/mo. English: IELTS 6.0–6.5. Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $8,000. Minimum English: IELTS 6 or equal.\nFacts: tuition EUR 1,500–7,000/yr for English-taught bachelor's · medicine EUR 12,000–17,000 · private universities EUR 9,000–27,000; living EUR 700–1,200/month · proof of funds EUR 650/month; intakes September/October; processing National D visa, then a residence permit within 90 days of arriving; language Greek for most bachelor's; more English-taught master's.\nExample universities: National and Kapodistrian University of Athens, Aristotle University of Thessaloniki, National Technical University of Athens, Athens University of Economics and Business, University of Patras, University of Crete. About 24+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Business & Economics, Archaeology & Classics, Shipping & Maritime, Medicine, Computer Science.\nVisa: National Type D Visa (Studies), then a residence permit. Steps: 1) Get admitted and pay any required tuition 2) Get a police certificate and a medical certificate 3) Show at least EUR 650 a month and health insurance 4) Apply for the National D visa at the Greek Embassy in Addis Ababa 5) Apply for the residence permit within 90 days of arriving\nPaid or shown before the visa: Proof of at least €650 a month, health insurance, and your tuition if your program charges it.\nExtra documents and health checks: A police certificate and a medical certificate for the visa.\nVisa tip: Check every Ministry of Education and Foreign Affairs stamp on your documents, book real accommodation, and know your course well for the interview.\nWork while studying (good): Part-time work up to 20 hours a week. There is no special job-search permit after graduating; you need a job offer or further study to stay.\nEarnings: Minimum wage €920 a month, paid 14 times a year, in 2026. At 20 hours a week, roughly €530 a month before tax.\nStaying after graduation: very limited (Job offer needed).\nTips: Check that your program is fully taught in English before you apply. Bring a police certificate and a medical certificate for the visa.\nScholarships: Erasmus Mundus Joint Masters.\n\n## Spain\nSummary: Work up to 30 hours a week while you study. Spain's public universities are affordable, and more than 800 degrees are taught fully or partly in English. Students can work up to 30 hours a week, and graduates can get a 12-month permit to look for a job. For most bachelor's degrees, non-EU students first need their Grade 12 certificate recognised (homologación).\nKey point: Students can work up to 30 hours a week, and graduates get 12 months to look for a job.\nTuition: €750–2,500/yr public. Living: €800–1,300/mo. English: IELTS 6.0–6.5 / Spanish B2. Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $10,000. Minimum English: IELTS 6 or equal.\nFacts: tuition Public universities about EUR 750–2,500/yr in most regions; non-EU students pay more in some regions; living EUR 800–1,300/month · proof of funds EUR 600/month; intakes September; processing Apply at least 2 months before your course; decisions take about a month; language Spanish for most bachelor's; 300+ bachelor's fully in English.\nExample universities: University of Barcelona, Autonomous University of Madrid, Complutense University of Madrid, Pompeu Fabra University, Carlos III University of Madrid, University of Valencia. About 95+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Business & Management, Tourism & Hospitality, Engineering, Computer Science.\nVisa: Long-stay student visa (Embassy of Spain, Addis Ababa). Steps: 1) Get admitted; for most bachelor's, get your Grade 12 certificate recognised (homologación) and apply through UNEDasiss 2) Show EUR 600 a month for your stay, not counting tuition 3) Get private health insurance with no co-payments 4) Get a police certificate and a medical certificate, legalised and translated into Spanish 5) Apply at the Embassy of Spain in Addis Ababa at least 2 months before your course\nPaid or shown before the visa: Proof of €600 a month (€7,200 for a year), not counting tuition, plus private health insurance with no co-payments.\nExtra documents and health checks: A police certificate (Certificate of Good Conduct) and a medical certificate, both legalised and translated into Spanish.\nVisa tip: Check every Ministry of Education and Foreign Affairs stamp on your documents, book real accommodation, and know your course well for the interview.\nWork while studying (strong): Up to 30 hours a week without a separate work permit, as long as it fits around your classes. After graduating, a 12-month permit lets you look for a job.\nEarnings: Minimum wage €1,381 a month full-time (€1,221 paid 14 times a year). At 30 hours a week, roughly €1,000 a month before tax.\nStaying after graduation: good (12 months).\nGrade 12 route: Spain first needs your Grade 12 certificate recognised (homologación), which can take months.\nTips: Start the recognition (homologación) of your Grade 12 certificate early. It can take months. Your police certificate and medical certificate must be legalised and translated into Spanish.\nScholarships: Erasmus Mundus Joint Masters.\n\n## Netherlands\nSummary: Many English-taught degrees and a 1-year orientation permit. The Netherlands has one of Europe's largest choices of English-taught degrees. Non-EU students pay higher tuition, usually about €13,000 to €20,000 a year for a bachelor's. Your university applies for your residence permit, and graduates can get a one-year orientation permit to work or start a business.\nKey point: One of Europe's largest choices of English-taught degrees, and a one-year orientation permit after graduating.\nTuition: €13,000–20,000/yr. Living: €1,000–1,500/mo. English: IELTS 6.0–6.5. Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $23,000. Minimum English: IELTS 6.5 or equal.\nFacts: tuition Non-EU bachelor's usually EUR 13,000–20,000/yr; living EUR 1,000–1,500/month · proof of funds EUR 1,130.77/month (2026); intakes September · some February; processing The university applies to the IND for you; language Most international programs are in English.\nExample universities: Delft University of Technology, University of Amsterdam, Wageningen University & Research, Utrecht University, University of Groningen, University of Twente. About 50+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Engineering, Business & Economics, Agriculture & Food, Data Science, Psychology.\nVisa: MVV entry visa and residence permit (requested by your university). Steps: 1) Get admitted; universities of applied sciences are often more open to a Grade 12 certificate 2) Pay the first year of tuition and send proof of funds to your university 3) The university applies to the IND for your entry visa and permit 4) Collect the MVV entry visa at the Dutch embassy 5) Take the TB test at the local health service (GGD) within 3 months of arriving\nPaid or shown before the visa: Your university applies for the permit. You show €1,130.77 a month (€13,569 a year) plus tuition, and pay the IND fee, about €254, usually through the university.\nExtra documents and health checks: A TB test (chest X-ray) at the local health service (GGD) within 3 months of arriving, about €60.\nVisa tip: Your university handles the visa request, so reply quickly and send every document they ask for.\nWork while studying (limited): Up to 16 hours a week, or full-time in June, July and August, but not both. Your employer needs a work permit (TWV) for you. After graduating, a one-year orientation permit lets you work or start a business without a work permit.\nEarnings: Minimum wage about €14.70 an hour in 2026. 16 hours a week is roughly €1,000 a month before tax.\nStaying after graduation: strong (12 months).\nGrade 12 route: Many universities here ask for a year of university study in Ethiopia after Grade 12, or a preparation course.\nTips: Research universities often ask for a year of university study after Grade 12. Universities of applied sciences can be more open. Housing is hard to find. Start looking as soon as you are admitted.\nScholarships: NL Scholarship (Netherlands), Erasmus Mundus Joint Masters.\n\n## Taiwan\nSummary: Strong in technology, with up to 2 years to stay after graduating. Taiwan is strong in engineering, technology and science, with many English-taught master's programs and the TaiwanICDF scholarship open to Ethiopians. Students can work up to 20 hours a week with a permit, and since 2026 graduates can stay up to 2 years and work without a separate permit. There is no Taiwan office in Ethiopia, so the visa is handled by the offices in Somaliland or Saudi Arabia.\nKey point: Since 2026, graduates can stay up to 2 years and work without a separate work permit.\nTuition: US$3,100–4,200/yr. Living: NT$28,550/mo (Taipei). English: IELTS 5.5–6.5. Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $10,000. Minimum English: IELTS 5.5 or equal.\nFacts: tuition National Taiwan University bachelor's about US$1,550–2,070 per semester; living About NT$28,550/month in Taipei (Ministry of Education estimate); intakes September · some February; processing Apply through the Taiwan office in Somaliland or Saudi Arabia; language Many English-taught master's; Mandarin for most bachelor's.\nExample universities: National Taiwan University, National Tsing Hua University, National Yang Ming Chiao Tung University, National Cheng Kung University, National Taiwan University of Science and Technology, National Chengchi University. About 135+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Electrical & Computer Engineering, Semiconductors, Computer Science, Agriculture, Public Health.\nVisa: Resident Visa for study (Taiwan office in Somaliland or Saudi Arabia). Steps: 1) Get admitted by a Taiwanese university 2) Prepare your authenticated diploma and transcripts 3) Contact the Taiwan office responsible for Ethiopia, in Somaliland or Saudi Arabia 4) Apply for the resident visa; a police certificate may be asked for 5) Apply for your Alien Resident Certificate after arriving\nExtra documents and health checks: A police certificate may be asked for, case by case.\nVisa tip: There is no Taiwan office in Ethiopia. Contact the offices responsible for Ethiopia, in Somaliland or Saudi Arabia, early.\nWork while studying (good): Up to 20 hours a week during semesters with a work permit, and no limit in summer and winter breaks. Since 2026, graduates can stay up to 2 years and work without a separate work permit.\nEarnings: Minimum wage NT$196 an hour in 2026 (NT$205 in 2027). At 20 hours a week, roughly NT$17,000 a month before tax.\nStaying after graduation: strong (Up to 2 years).\nTips: Learn some Mandarin. It helps in daily life and with part-time jobs. Apply for the TaiwanICDF scholarship between 1 December and 15 March.\nScholarships: TaiwanICDF Scholarship.\n\n## Romania\nSummary: English-taught medicine at a lower cost. Romania is popular for six-year medicine and dentistry degrees taught in English, at lower fees than most of the EU. Your university sends your file to the Ministry of Education, which issues the Letter of Acceptance you need for the visa. Students can work up to 6 hours a day, and graduates can stay 9 months to look for a job or start a business.\nKey point: English-taught medicine at lower fees than most of Europe. Places are limited, so apply early.\nTuition: €3,000–8,500/yr. Living: €300–700/mo. English: B2 English (IELTS 5.5–6.0). Lowest realistic yearly budget for tuition and living: about $8,000. Minimum English: IELTS 5.5 or equal.\nFacts: tuition English-taught medicine about EUR 7,000–8,500/yr · other fields about EUR 3,000–3,600/yr; living EUR 300–700/month; intakes October (apply in spring or summer); processing The Ministry's Letter of Acceptance can take up to about 8 weeks, then the visa; language English or French for many medicine programs; Romanian for most others, after a preparatory year.\nExample universities: Carol Davila University of Medicine and Pharmacy, Iuliu Hațieganu University of Medicine and Pharmacy, Grigore T. Popa University of Medicine and Pharmacy Iași, Victor Babeș University of Medicine and Pharmacy Timișoara, University of Medicine and Pharmacy of Craiova, Babeș-Bolyai University. About 85+ universities in total.\nPopular fields: Medicine, Dentistry, Pharmacy, Engineering, Computer Science.\nVisa: Long-stay study visa (Embassy of Romania, Addis Ababa). Steps: 1) Apply to the university; it sends your file to the Ministry of Education 2) Receive the Ministry's Letter of Acceptance 3) Pay the first-year tuition by the university's deadline 4) Get a police certificate and health insurance, and show living funds 5) Apply for the study visa at the Embassy of Romania in Addis Ababa, then for the residence permit after arriving\nPaid or shown before the visa: Your full first-year tuition, paid after the Ministry's Letter of Acceptance, plus living funds of at least the Romanian minimum wage for each month.\nExtra documents and health checks: A police certificate for the visa, plus the Ministry of Education's Letter of Acceptance.\nVisa tip: Check every Ministry of Education and Foreign Affairs stamp on your documents, book real accommodation, and know your course well for the interview.\nWork while studying (good): Students can work part-time up to 6 hours a day without a work permit. After graduating, your residence can be extended by 9 months to look for a job or start a business.\nEarnings: Minimum wage RON 4,325 a month full-time from July 2026. At 6 hours a day, roughly RON 3,200 a month before tax.\nStaying after graduation: good (9 months).\nTips: Places in English-taught medicine are limited and fill up fast, so apply early. Pay your first-year tuition by the deadline after the Letter of Acceptance. Some deadlines are only a few days.\nScholarships: Romanian Government Scholarship, Erasmus Mundus Joint Masters.\n\n# SCHOLARSHIPS\n## Mastercard Foundation Scholars Program\nLevels: ba, ma (ba = bachelor's, ma = master's, phd = PhD). Coverage: full. Covers: Tuition, housing, living costs, travel, leadership support.\nWhen to apply: Varies by partner university; many Aug–Jan. Who can apply: Academically talented young Africans; With financial need; Committed to giving back.\nNote: You apply through each partner university.\nOfficial site: https://mastercardfdn.org\n\n## Stipendium Hungaricum\nLevels: ba, ma, phd (ba = bachelor's, ma = master's, phd = PhD). Coverage: full. Covers: Tuition, monthly stipend, housing, insurance.\nWhen to apply: Usually Nov–mid Jan. Who can apply: Grade 12 (bachelor's) or a degree (master's/PhD); Nominated by the Ethiopian sending authority; English test for English programs.\nNote: Places are given through each country's sending partner, so apply early and follow both steps.\nOfficial site: https://stipendiumhungaricum.hu\n\n## Chinese Government Scholarship (CSC)\nLevels: ba, ma, phd (ba = bachelor's, ma = master's, phd = PhD). Coverage: full. Covers: Tuition, accommodation, stipend, insurance.\nWhen to apply: Usually Nov–Mar; some universities accept until April. Who can apply: Under 25 (bachelor's), 35 (master's), 40 (PhD); Good grades and health.\nAge limits: {\"ba\":25,\"ma\":35,\"phd\":40}.\nNote: Apply via the embassy route or directly to universities.\nOfficial site: https://www.campuschina.org\n\n## Türkiye Scholarships (Türkiye Bursları)\nLevels: ba, ma, phd (ba = bachelor's, ma = master's, phd = PhD). Coverage: full. Covers: Tuition, monthly stipend, housing, insurance, flight and a Turkish course.\nWhen to apply: Once a year, usually Jan–Feb. Who can apply: Bachelor's: under 21 with good Grade 12 results; Master's under 30, PhD under 35; Not already studying at a Turkish university.\nAge limits: {\"ba\":21,\"ma\":30,\"phd\":35}.\nNote: There is no application fee. Interviews usually follow a few months later.\nOfficial site: https://www.turkiyeburslari.gov.tr\n\n## Chevening Scholarship\nLevels: ma (ba = bachelor's, ma = master's, phd = PhD). Coverage: full. Covers: One-year master's: tuition, living, flights, visa.\nWhen to apply: Opens early August, closes early October. Who can apply: Bachelor's degree; At least two years (2,800 hours) of work experience; Return home for at least two years after your studies.\nNote: Leadership and clear career plans count as much as grades.\nOfficial site: https://www.chevening.org\n\n## DAAD Development-Related Postgraduate Courses (EPOS)\nLevels: ma, phd (ba = bachelor's, ma = master's, phd = PhD). Coverage: full. Covers: Monthly stipend, travel and insurance; most courses charge no tuition.\nWhen to apply: Varies by course; many Aug–Oct, a year ahead. Who can apply: Bachelor's degree in a related field; At least two years of work experience.\nNote: You apply to the course, not to DAAD. You can pick up to three courses.\nOfficial site: https://www.daad.de/epos-info\n\n## Global Korea Scholarship (GKS)\nLevels: ba, ma, phd (ba = bachelor's, ma = master's, phd = PhD). Coverage: full. Covers: Tuition, flights, monthly allowance, a Korean language year and insurance.\nWhen to apply: Graduate: usually Feb–Mar · Undergraduate: Sep–Oct. Who can apply: Under 40 for graduate, under 25 for undergraduate; GPA of 80% or in the top 20% of your class; Documents notarised by MOFA and the Korean Embassy.\nAge limits: {\"ba\":25,\"ma\":40,\"phd\":40}.\nNote: Embassy-track places for Ethiopia are limited, so a strong study plan and recommendation letters matter.\nOfficial site: https://www.studyinkorea.go.kr\n\n## Erasmus Mundus Joint Masters\nLevels: ma (ba = bachelor's, ma = master's, phd = PhD). Coverage: full. Covers: Tuition, travel, monthly allowance in 2+ European countries.\nWhen to apply: Usually Oct–Jan. Who can apply: Bachelor's degree or final year; Strong academic record; English at B2 or above.\nNote: Each program sets its own deadline; apply to up to three.\nOfficial site: https://www.eacea.ec.europa.eu/scholarships/erasmus-mundus-catalogue_en\n\n## Fulbright Foreign Student Program\nLevels: ma, phd (ba = bachelor's, ma = master's, phd = PhD). Coverage: full. Covers: Tuition, living stipend, travel, health insurance.\nWhen to apply: Announced by the U.S. Embassy. Who can apply: Bachelor's with strong grades; Strong English; Commitment to return.\nNote: Very competitive. A strong essay and interview make the difference.\nOfficial site: https://foreign.fulbrightonline.org\n\n## ICCR India-Africa Maitri Scholarship\nLevels: ba, ma, phd (ba = bachelor's, ma = master's, phd = PhD). Coverage: full. Covers: Tuition, monthly stipend, housing allowance and return flight.\nWhen to apply: Usually Feb–Apr on the A2A portal. Who can apply: Grade 12 or a degree, depending on the level; Good English; Medical fitness certificate.\nNote: You choose up to five universities on the A2A portal. Places are set for each country every year.\nOfficial site: https://a2ascholarships.iccr.gov.in\n\n## France Excellence Eiffel Scholarship\nLevels: ma, phd (ba = bachelor's, ma = master's, phd = PhD). Coverage: full. Covers: Monthly allowance, return flight, insurance and housing help.\nWhen to apply: Through your French university, usually Oct–Dec. Who can apply: Applying to a French master's or PhD; Nominated by the French university; Excellent academic record.\nNote: You cannot apply directly. Your French university nominates you, and many set internal deadlines in October or November.\nOfficial site: https://www.campusfrance.org/en/eiffel-scholarship-program-of-excellence\n\n## Russian Government Scholarship\nLevels: ba, ma, phd (ba = bachelor's, ma = master's, phd = PhD). Coverage: full. Covers: Tuition incl. prep year, small stipend, dormitory.\nWhen to apply: Usually September to mid-January on education-in-russia.com. Who can apply: Grade 12 or a degree; Selected through the quota for Ethiopia.\nNote: Most programs are in Russian after a language year.\nOfficial site: https://www.education-in-russia.com\n\n## Italy regional study grant (DSU)\nLevels: ba, ma (ba = bachelor's, ma = master's, phd = PhD). Coverage: partial. Covers: Tuition waiver, yearly cash grant, often housing and meals.\nWhen to apply: Usually Jul–Sep, after admission. Who can apply: Admitted to an Italian public university; Family income below the regional limit; Income documents legalised in Ethiopia.\nNote: Many Ethiopian students in Italy study on DSU. The paperwork decides it.\n\n## Erhard Busek Grant (Austria)\nLevels: ma (ba = bachelor's, ma = master's, phd = PhD). Coverage: full. Covers: €1,300 a month for 24 months and up to €1,200 for travel; the university waives tuition.\nWhen to apply: Nominated by an Austrian university; applications from September. Who can apply: Bachelor's degree from a developing country; Under 35; Admitted to a two-year master's in Austria, often STEM or English-taught.\nAge limits: {\"ma\":35}.\nNote: You cannot apply directly. The Austrian university must nominate you.\nOfficial site: https://oead.at/en/to-austria/grants-and-scholarships/erhard-busek-grant\n\n## Ignacy Łukasiewicz Scholarship (Poland)\nLevels: ma (ba = bachelor's, ma = master's, phd = PhD). Coverage: full. Covers: Free master's study at a public university and a monthly stipend.\nWhen to apply: Announced in the first quarter of each year. Who can apply: Citizen of a listed developing country, including Ethiopia; Master's in engineering, technical, agricultural or natural sciences; Public universities only.\nNote: Follow the embassy's Study in Poland! Ethiopia Telegram channel for the call.\nOfficial site: https://nawa.gov.pl/en/students/foreign-students/the-ignacy-lukasiewicz-scholarship-programme\n\n## NL Scholarship (Netherlands)\nLevels: ba, ma (ba = bachelor's, ma = master's, phd = PhD). Coverage: partial. Covers: €5,000 one time, in your first year.\nWhen to apply: Usually by 1 February or 1 May, depending on the university. Who can apply: Non-EEA nationality; Applying to a participating Dutch university; Strong grades.\nNote: It is a partial grant. You still need funds for tuition and living.\nOfficial site: https://www.studyinnl.org/nl-scholarship\n\n## TaiwanICDF Scholarship\nLevels: ba, ma, phd (ba = bachelor's, ma = master's, phd = PhD). Coverage: full. Covers: Tuition, housing, a monthly stipend, insurance and return flights.\nWhen to apply: Usually 1 December to 15 March, online. Who can apply: Citizen of a listed partner country, including Ethiopia; Admitted to a partner program in Taiwan; Recommended by the Taiwan office responsible for your country.\nNote: Most programs are master's and PhD, taught in English.\nOfficial site: https://www.icdf.org.tw\n\n## Romanian Government Scholarship\nLevels: ba, ma, phd (ba = bachelor's, ma = master's, phd = PhD). Coverage: full. Covers: Tuition, a Romanian language year, a monthly allowance and, where available, a dorm place.\nWhen to apply: Usually mid-February to 31 March, online. Who can apply: Non-EU citizen with good grades; Ready to study in Romanian after a preparatory year; Not for medicine, dentistry or pharmacy.\nNote: Bachelor's and master's on this scholarship are taught in Romanian.\nOfficial site: https://scholarships.studyinromania.gov.ro\n\n# ADVISOR EXPERIENCE (Yimaru's advisors)\nThese notes come from Yimaru's advisors. They are practical guidance, not official rules. Percentages and waiting times are rough estimates. When you use them, say they are from our advisors' experience and are not a guarantee.\n## Admission: how easy it is to get a university offer (rough estimates, for students who meet the requirements)\nItaly about 95%, China about 95%, Russia about 95%, Hungary about 85%, Türkiye about 80%, Austria about 80%, France about 80%, Greece about 80%, Poland about 80%, Spain about 70%, Canada about 65%, USA about 65%, Netherlands about 65%, UK about 60%, Taiwan about 60%. These are chances of getting admitted, not chances of getting the visa.\n\n## Grade and entry requirements\n- Most universities in Europe and North America want a Grade 12 result that would let you enter university in Ethiopia (usually at least 50%).\n- Germany and Austria usually also want one year of university study in Ethiopia, or a preparation year abroad (Studienkolleg in Germany).\n- For a master's degree, most countries ask for a CGPA of about 2.5 to 3.0 out of 4.0.\n- Medicine, dentistry and pharmacy are the hardest to get into. Architecture and nursing also need extra support.\n- For medicine, Russia, China, Romania, Hungary and private universities in Türkiye admit students mainly on biology and chemistry results and an interview.\n- Universities rarely reject students for age or a gap in studies. The real test is the embassy visa. Scholarships do have age limits (for example Türkiye Bursları: under 21 for bachelor's, under 30 for master's).\n\n## English tests\n- IELTS Academic and TOEFL iBT are accepted everywhere we work with.\n- Duolingo English Test is accepted in the USA, Canada, many UK universities, some Italian universities (for example Bologna, Politecnico di Torino, Padua), Hungary, Spain and France. German public universities and Dutch universities generally do not accept it.\n- Some universities accept a Medium of Instruction (MOI) letter from your Ethiopian school or university instead of a test.\n\n## Visa: how hard it usually is for Ethiopian students (advisor experience)\n- Usually easier: Russia, China, India, Malaysia, Italy (when documents and CIMEA/Declaration of Value are complete), Germany (with blocked account and admission), Austria, France (after Campus France approval).\n- Medium: UK (high if the CAS and the 28-day bank rule are met, lower if the credibility interview goes badly), Hungary, Spain, Greece, Netherlands, Türkiye.\n- Hard, with strict checks: USA (many bachelor's students refused under Section 214(b)), Canada (many refusals over funds and home ties), Poland (very strict interview and high refusal rate), Taiwan.\n\n## Why visas are refused, and how to avoid it\n- USA (214(b)): not showing you will return to Ethiopia, memorised interview answers, or doubts about the sponsor. Avoid it by explaining clearly how the degree helps your career in Ethiopia and practising natural answers.\n- Canada: sudden large deposits with no explained source, a weak study plan (SOP), weak ties to Ethiopia. Avoid it with 6 months of clean bank history plus trade licence, tax records or property documents, paying first-year tuition in advance, and a strong SOP.\n- Italy and Schengen countries (Germany, Austria, Poland): missing or unauthenticated education documents, fake or unconfirmed accommodation, and not knowing your own course at the interview. Avoid it by checking every Ministry of Education and Ministry of Foreign Affairs stamp, booking real accommodation and studying your course details.\n- UK: the bank balance going below the required amount even for one day in the 28-day period, or failing the UKVI credibility interview. Check the daily balance before printing the statement and do a practice interview.\n\n## Never say this at a visa interview\n- \"I will work there to pay my fees or support my family.\" (It sounds like you are going to work, not study.)\n- \"My agent chose my university and course for me\", or not knowing your city, university or course.\n- \"I will stay there after my studies and get citizenship.\" (In the USA, Canada and UK this often leads to refusal.)\n\n## Sponsors other than parents\n- UK: bank statements from uncles, aunts, brothers or sisters are not accepted. Only the student, parents, a legal guardian or a spouse can be the source of funds.\n- USA, Canada, Italy, Germany, Austria and France: a relative can sponsor, but strong proof is needed: birth certificates showing the family link; a notarised affidavit of support (in Germany a Verpflichtungserklärung from the local authority, in Austria a Haftungserklärung); the sponsor's passport or residence card, 3 to 6 months of bank statements, tax records and an employment letter; and proof the sponsor can support their own family too.\n\n## Documents that strengthen an application\n- The sponsor's renewed trade licence and tax clearance.\n- Property documents (house title deed, car ownership book). These show strong ties to Ethiopia, which matters most for Canada, the USA and the UK.\n- Parents' employment and salary letters with pension or tax records.\n- A letter and proof explaining any large deposit (for example a house or car sale contract, or business income).\n\n## Missing documents that lead to refusal\n- Education documents not authenticated by the Ministry of Education and the Ministry of Foreign Affairs.\n- For Italy: missing CIMEA statement or Declaration of Value.\n- A fake bank statement or unconfirmed accommodation booking.\n\n## After a visa refusal\n- USA: you can apply again quickly (2 to 4 weeks later), but without a real change in your situation or interview, it is usually refused again.\n- UK and Canada: fix the reason written in the refusal letter (for example the bank dates or the SOP) and apply again; many succeed within 2 to 6 weeks.\n- Italy and other Schengen countries: you can appeal within about 30 to 60 days, or complete the missing documents or funds and apply for the next intake.\n- After two refusals for the same country (for example Poland or the USA), switching to another country such as Italy, Austria or Malaysia often works better.\n\n## Embassy appointment waiting times in Addis Ababa (rough, advisor experience)\nItaly 3 to 8 weeks (very busy June to August); Germany 4 to 10 weeks (register on the waiting list early); USA 2 to 6 weeks (an expedited appointment may be possible if classes start within 60 days); UK 1 to 3 weeks; France: Campus France interview 3 to 5 weeks plus visa appointment 1 to 2 weeks; Türkiye, Russia, China, India 1 to 3 weeks.\n\n## Countries where part of the process may happen outside Ethiopia (advisor experience, confirm each case)\nIreland, Portugal, the Baltic countries, Finland, Denmark and Norway often involve the embassy in Nairobi. Australia and New Zealand: biometrics in Addis Ababa, the case is handled elsewhere. Taiwan: there is no Taiwan office in Ethiopia; visas go through its offices in Somaliland or Saudi Arabia.\n\n## How long the whole process takes (from first meeting to travel)\n- Faster (3 to 5 months): Türkiye, Malaysia, Russia, China, UK.\n- Medium (5 to 8 months): USA, Canada, France, Hungary, Netherlands.\n- Longer, many documents (7 to 11 months): Italy, Germany, Austria.\n- The biggest delays: getting or renewing a passport in Ethiopia (often 1 to 3 months), getting official transcripts from Ethiopian universities, CIMEA or Declaration of Value for Italy (2 to 4 months), and the June to August embassy rush.\n\n## Best time to start\nThe September/October (fall) intake is best: most European and North American universities open all their programs then.\n\n## Low-budget routes (advisor experience, yearly totals for tuition, dorm and food)\nIndia about $3,200 to $4,500; Russia outside big cities about $3,500 to $4,700; China with a partial tuition waiver about $3,500 to $4,500; Türkiye public universities outside Istanbul about $3,800 to $4,800. Italy: a student who wins a regional scholarship (DSU or ER.GO) gets free tuition, about €6,000 to €7,500 a year and free meals, so the main cost is the first trip, document authentication and the first month's rent (about $2,500 to $3,500).\n\n## Chinese Government Scholarship (CSC) and MOFCOM\nUsually GPA 3.0 or higher; age under 25 for bachelor's and under 35 for master's; an MOI letter is often accepted; MOFCOM prefers at least 2 years of work experience.\n\n## What makes students succeed\nFrom our advisors' experience: starting early, getting the money ready properly, and keeping every document clean, complete and authentic.\n";
